# Seeded files: <repo path>  <system path>
#
# Files an application OWNS and rewrites, which nonetheless have to exist before that
# application has ever run. Copied once if the destination is absent, and never touched
# again.
#
# This is a third category, and the distinction from the other two is the point:
#
#   links   the repo owns the file; the system points at it; edits are live
#   copies  root owns the path; the repo cannot write it, so drift is DIFFED and reported
#   seeds   the app owns the file; the repo supplies a starting point and then lets go
#
# Seeds are deliberately NOT diffed. Their drift is the application working as intended,
# and flagging it every run would train the eye to skip the section -- the same argument
# hosts/caesar/bootstrap/copies makes for leaving Noctalia's settings.toml out.
#
# The bar for adding a row here: something must FAIL, or come up WRONG, if the file is
# missing at first login. If nothing does, the file does not need seeding and this is just
# a stale copy nobody asked for. (Noctalia's settings clear the second half of that bar:
# absent, the shell starts at its defaults. They are seeded per host --
# hosts/caesar/bootstrap/seeds -- and their drift IS checked, by noctalia-snapshot --check,
# in the other direction: not "has the app changed the file" but "does the repo still
# capture what the app has".)
common/umbriel/noctalia.toml	~/.config/umbriel/noctalia.toml
# Authored config for apps that REWRITE their own file (manifest §8 D) -- each tested
# 2026-09-28 against a symlinked copy, which is what decided seed over link:
#   btop        rewrites the whole file when its header names another btop version, i.e.
#               on every upgrade: 15 authored lines became 286, the comments gone (in
#               place -- through a link it would have rewritten the repo's copy)
#   herdr       writes its config in place (onboarding, its settings, config reset-keys)
#   ai-usagebar saves settings by writing a temp file and RENAMING it over the path: a
#               link there is silently replaced by a regular file, the sed -i failure
common/btop/btop.conf		~/.config/btop/btop.conf
common/herdr/config.toml	~/.config/herdr/config.toml
common/ai-usagebar/config.toml	~/.config/ai-usagebar/config.toml
